250 spearmen (lanceros de Artigas), men and women, enslaved soldiers",\n      "settlements": "Kamba Cuá, Kamba Kokue",\n      "legacy": "African traditional ballet; central cultural identity of Afro-Paraguayan community"\n    }\n  },\n\n[kamba]\n"society_and_culture": {\n    "origin_myth": {\n      "narrative": "Mulungu created a man and woman from heaven, placed them on a rock at Nzaui (footprints visible). Mulungu caused rainfall; from anthills emerged a man and woman (initiators of Aimu/spirits clan). Heavenly couple had sons; anthill couple had daughters. Marriage occurred via dowry. Prosperity led to forgetting Mulungu; famine dispersed the family. Some became Kikuyu, others Meru, some remained Akamba.",\n      "significance": "Explains GEMA common origin; ritual footprints at Nzaui"\n    },\n    "family_structure": {\n      "homestead": "Musyi",\n      "clan": "Mbai (extended family/clan)",\n      "head": "Man (Nau, Tata, or Asa)",\n      "womens_role": "Controls own plot of land (given upon marriage); primary food producer; raises children; Mwaitu (\'our One\') commands adult children\'s deference",\n      "grandparents": "Susu/Usua (grandmother), Umau/Umaa (grandfather); assist with rope-making, leather tanning, beehive carving, stools, calabash decoration, bows/arrows",\n      "avuncular_relationships": {\n        "maternal_uncle": "Inaimiwa",\n        "maternal_aunt": "Mwendya",\n        "paternal_uncle/aunt": "Mwendw\'au",\n        "paternal_cousins (male)": "Mwanaasa / Mwanaa\'ia",\n        "paternal_cousins (female)": "Mwiitu wa\'asa / Mwiitu wa\'ia",\n        "maternal_cousins (male)": "Mwanaa mwendya",\n        "maternal_cousins (female)": "Mwiitu wa mwendya"\n      },\n      "child_rearing": "Children move freely between households; aunts/uncles function as de facto parents"\n    },\n    "clans": [\n      "Anzauni",\n      "Aombe",\n      "Akitondo",\n      "Amwei (Angwina)",\n      "Atwii",\n      "Amumui",\n      "Aethanga",\n      "Atangwa",\n      "Amutei",\n      "Aewani",\n      "Akitutu",\n      "Ambua",\n      "Aiini",\n      "Asii",\n      "Akiimi",\n      "Amũũti",\n      "Amũũnda"\n    ],\n    "naming_system": {\n      "first_four_children": [\n        "First boy: paternal grandfather",\n        "Second boy: maternal grandfather",\n        "First girl: paternal grandmother",\n        "Second girl: maternal grandmother"\n      ],\n      "avoidance_kinship": "Daughter-in-law cannot address parents-in-law by first name; given family name (e.g., \'Syomunyithya/ng\'a Mutunga\' meaning \'mother of Munyithya/Mutunga\') so that calling her son invokes father-in-law respectfully",\n      "circumstantial_names": {\n        "Nduku/Katuku (f), Mutuku/Kituku (m)": "Born at night",\n        "Kiloko (f), Kioko (m)": "Born in the morning",\n        "Mumbua/Syombua/Mbula (f), Wambua/Mbua (m)": "Born during rain",\n        "Wayua (f)": "Born during famine",\n        "Makau (m)": "Born during war",\n        "Kasyoka/Kasyoki (f), Musyoka/Kasyuko/Musyoki (m)": "Reincarnation of deceased family member",\n        "Mutua/Mutui (m), Mutuo/Mwikali (f)": "Long-awaited child or prolonged gestation",\n        "Munyao (m)": "Born during famine",\n        "Mueni/Waeni (f)": "Born during time of visitors",\n        "Maundu (m)": "Born during time of multiple activities/things",\n        "Muthami/Muthama (m)": "Born during migration"\n      },\n      "aspirational_names": [\n        "Mutongoi (leader)",\n        "Musili (judge)",\n        "Muthui (rich one)",\n        "Ngumbau (hero, brave one)",\n        "Kitonga (wealthy one)",\n        "Musumbi (king; rare, no monarchical system; prophecy of man from Kitui, ancestry tracing to west)"\n      ],\n      "aesthetic_names_female": [\n        "Mumbe (beautiful one)",\n        "Mwende (beloved)"\n      ],\n      "animal_names": "Nzoka (snake), Mbiti (hyena), Mbuku (hare), Munyambu (lion), Mbiwa (fox), Ngiti (dog), Ng\'ombe (cow), Nguku (chicken) - given to children born after stillbirths to \'wish away bad omen\'"\n    },\n    "religion": {\n      "traditional": {\n        "supreme_being": ["Ngai", "Mulungu", "Asa", "Ngai Mumbi (God the Creator)", "Mwatuangi (God the finger-divider)"],\n        "attributes": "Monotheistic, invisible, transcendental, lives in sky (yayayani/ituni), omnipotent, merciful, distant",\n        "intercessors": "Aimu/Maimu (spirits of departed); remembered in family rituals, offerings, libations at individual altars",\n        "rain_making": "Kilumi dance; healing rite to restore environmental balance; spiritual essence distinguishes African-derived dances globally; involves symbolic movement, offering, prayers",\n        "kilumi_context": "Present since beginning of Kamba existence; ritual intervention for drought"\n      },\n      "contemporary": "Overwhelmingly Christian (various denominations, with strong Anglican, Catholic, and African Instituted Church presence); Muslim minority",\n      "notable_theologians": "John Samuel Mbiti (father of modern African Theology)"\n    },\n    "music_and_dance": {\n      "dance_styles": [\n        {\n          "name": "Mwali (pl. Myali)",\n          "purpose": "Criticize anti-social behavior, social commentary"\n        },\n        {\n          "name": "Kilumi",\n          "purpose": "Religious, healing, rain-making ceremonies"\n        },\n        {\n          "name": "Ngoma",\n          "purpose": "Religious dances"\n        },\n        {\n          "name": "Mwilu",\n          "purpose": "Circumcision dance"\n        },\n        {\n          "name": "Mbalya / Ngutha",\n          "purpose": "Young people\'s entertainment after chores; love songs, satirical numbers by dance leader"\n        },\n        {\n          "name": "Kamandiko",\n          "purpose": "Modern disco, typically post-wedding"\n        }\n      ],\n      "musical_characteristics": {\n        "scale": "Pentatonic",\n        "singing": "Lively, tuneful",\n        "song_subjects": "Marriage, birth, national occasions