",\n          "purpose": "Criticize anti-social behavior, social commentary"\n        },\n        {\n          "name": "Kilumi",\n          "purpose": "Religious, healing, rain-making ceremonies"\n        },\n        {\n          "name": "Ngoma",\n          "purpose": "Religious dances"\n        },\n        {\n          "name": "Mwilu",\n          "purpose": "Circumcision dance"\n        },\n        {\n          "name": "Mbalya / Ngutha",\n          "purpose": "Young people\'s entertainment after chores; love songs, satirical numbers by dance leader"\n        },\n        {\n          "name": "Kamandiko",\n          "purpose": "Modern disco, typically post-wedding"\n        }\n      ],\n      "musical_characteristics": {\n        "scale": "Pentatonic",\n        "singing": "Lively, tuneful",\n        "song_subjects": "Marriage, birth, national occasions, satirical of deviant behavior, anti-social activity",\n        "work_songs": "Ngulu Mwelela (digging, labor)",\n        "herder_songs": "Distinct repertoire for herdsmen and boys"\n      },\n      "traditional_instruments": "Not extensively documented in source; rhythm emphasized over melody; drumming prominent",\n      "early_artist": "Mailu Mboo (Mwingi region, earliest documented traditional soloist)",\n      "contemporary_popular_musicians": [\n        "Ken Wamaria (rated top artist in Ukambani, \'richest Kenyan artist\' per Kioko, 2012)",\n        "Kativui",\n        "Kitunguu",\n        "Katombi",\n        "Maima",\n        "Vuusya Ungu"\n      ],\n      "benga_connection": "Kamba have contributed to benga genre; Kakai Kilonzo (1954–1987) of Les Kilimambogo Brothers was influential benga musician"\n    },\n    "clothing_and_adornment": {\n      "traditional_men": "Leather short kilts (animal skins/tree bark); copper/brass jewellery (neck-chains, bracelets, anklets); leather sandals (travel/market/visits); barefoot at home/fields",\n      "traditional_women": "Knee-length leather or bark skirts, beadwork embellishment; necklaces (beads from Swahili/Arab traders); shaved heads, beaded headbands (distinctive colors/patterns for kilumi groups)",\n      "contemporary": "Western/European dress; men: trousers, shirts; boys: shorts, short-sleeved shirts, t-shirts; women: dresses, skirts (customary), trousers, jeans, shorts; schoolchildren: shaved heads for uniformity/equality",\n      "footwear": "Leather sandals (traditional); modern footwear"\n    }\n  },', 'community': 'kamba', 'section': 'section_7', 'relevance': 1.3837733268737793, 'source': 'Kamba.txt'}, {'content': '"geography": {\n    "homeland": "Ukambani",\n    "constituent_counties": ["Machakos", "Kitui", "Makueni"],\n    "historical_heartland": "Mbooni Hills (second half of 17th century, first settlement)",\n    "topography": "Lowlands southeast Kenya, from Nairobi to Tsavo, northwards to Embu",\n    "neighbors": [\n      "Maasai (west, separated by Kenya-Uganda railway, Athi-River to Kibwezi)",\n      "Kikuyu (north)",\n      "Embu, Mbeere (north)",\n      "Taita (southeast)",\n      "Coastal Bantu (Kambe, Giriama, Mijikenda) (east)"\n    ],\n    "boundary_conflicts": "Historical cattle-rustling and pasture conflicts with Maasai on Konza plains; led to colonial creation of Cooperative Society, Konza, Potha, Malili Ranches; modern Konza Technopolis sits on these lands"\n  },', 'community': 'kamba', 'section': 'section_2', 'relevance': 1.53498113155365, 'source': 'Kamba.txt'}]}
