ols and weapons",\n          "Millet",\n          "Cattle",\n          "Medicinal products (miti, herbal remedies)"\n        ],\n        "trading_partners": [\n          "Kikuyu",\n          "Maasai",\n          "Meru",\n          "Embu",\n          "Mijikenda",\n          "Arab and Swahili coast traders"\n        ],\n        "role": "Middlemen between coast and interior; controlled central Kenyan trade corridors",\n        "economic_power": "Wielded economic control across central Kenya (Ki\'nyaa, \'Ostrich Country,\' derived from Kikamba reference to Mount Kenya\'s snow cap resembling male ostrich)"\n      },\n      "barter_system": "Prominent; offset food shortages from Ukambani droughts and famines"\n    },\n    "colonial_period": {\n      "arab_takeover": "Late 19th century: Arabs assumed coastal trade; Kamba became guides for caravans (ivory, precious stones, slaves)",\n      "explorer_guides": "Valued for geographical knowledge and neutral standing with interior societies",\n      "martial_race_discourse": {\n        "colonial_designation": "\'Premier martial race of Africa\', \'fighting race\', \'best soldierly material in Africa\', \'hardy, virile, courageous, mechanically-minded tribe\'",\n        "military_enlistment": "Kamba enlisted in King\'s African Rifles (KAR) at 3-4x their population percentage",\n        "context": "Press release by East Africa Command, early 1950s, during Mau Mau Emergency"\n      },\n      "resistance_movements": {\n        "nature": "Predominantly non-violent, peaceful protest",\n        "key_figures": [\n          "Syokimau (prophetess, healer)",\n          "Syotune wa Kathukye (led peaceful protest to recover confiscated cattle)",\n          "Muindi Mbingu (protest march to recover land/cattle, Mua Hills, Masaku; arrested)",\n          "Paul Ngei (joined Mau Mau; imprisoned Kapenguria)",\n          "JD Kali (joined Mau Mau; imprisoned Kapenguria)",\n          "Malu of Kilungu"\n        ],\n        "1938_livestock_control_legi